{"name":"Gentian Violet","hex":"#57487f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gentian violet","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Also known as crystal violet, a chemical dye used in the Gram stain test, and is also a fungicide. Chemically: hexamethyl pararosaniline chloride, C25H30ClN3","A violet colour similar to the gentian flower."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a violet colour similar to the gentian flower."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gentian_violet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crystal violet","blocks":[{"text":"Crystal violet or gentian violet, also known as methyl violet 10B or hexamethyl pararosaniline chloride, is a triarylmethane dye used as a histological stain and in Gram's method of classifying bacteria. Crystal violet has antibacterial, antifungal, and anthelmintic (vermicide) properties and was formerly important as a topical antiseptic. The medical use of the dye has been largely superseded by more modern drugs, although it is still listed by the World Health Organization."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Kristallviolett.svg/330px-Kristallviolett.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_violet","wikidata":"Q420705"},{"title":"Gentiana","blocks":[{"text":"Gentiana is a genus of flowering plants belonging to the gentian family (Gentianaceae), the tribe Gentianeae, and the monophyletic subtribe Gentianinae. With over 300 species, it is considered a large genus. Gentians are notable for their mostly large trumpet-shaped flowers, which are often of an intense blue hue, though some are also bright yellow. The type species of the genus is Gentiana lutea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Gentiana-verna2.jpg/330px-Gentiana-verna2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gentiana","wikidata":"Q144682"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)","wikidata":"Q428124"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Methyl_violet_10B_3D_ball.png/500px-Methyl_violet_10B_3D_ball.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Methyl_violet_10B_3D_ball.png/1280px-Methyl_violet_10B_3D_ball.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Methyl_violet_10B_3D_ball.png/500px-Methyl_violet_10B_3D_ball.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMethyl_violet_10B_3D_ball.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Sample_of_Crystal_Violet.jpg/500px-Sample_of_Crystal_Violet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Sample_of_Crystal_Violet.jpg/1280px-Sample_of_Crystal_Violet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Sample_of_Crystal_Violet.jpg/500px-Sample_of_Crystal_Violet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/